Q1. FunctionalClass <-> Symbol bridge
  ACCEPT only with this closed correspondence:
    ERIEC.Markers.FunctionalClass.consciousMarker
      <-> :conscious
    ERIEC.Markers.FunctionalClass.blindsightAnalog
      <-> :blindsight_analog
    ERIEC.Markers.FunctionalClass.nonconsciousMarker
      <-> :nonconscious
  Unknown Symbols must be rejected. The catalog name binding alone does not
  guarantee this semantic bridge. The implementation must provide one closed
  decoder/comparison boundary, while literal tests independently pin all three
  mappings. The manifest must state that this encoding is an assumption of the
  cross-language boundary. The exact proposition is the graph equation
  `classify markers = expectedClass`; unlike the former hinge Set/Bool mismatch,
  an independently supplied expected output makes the whole function graph
  decidable on the finite Bool domain.

Q2. Lean indexed Path <-> Julia OpenPath
  ACCEPT as witness_validator, not exact_finite_decision.
  Structural correspondence:
    Path.nil s <-> OpenPath([s], OpenEdgeKind[])
    Path.cons edge rest <-> prepend/append one adjacent state and its edge kind,
      with InternalEdge <-> EdgeKind.internal and
      CouplingEdge(port) <-> EdgeKind.coupling port.
  Lean's endpoint indices and embedded proof `G.step s kind m` are erased into a
  runtime state/kind list. OpenPath enforces nonempty states and the n/n-1 shape;
  check_open_path checks finite-carrier membership and recomputes graph.step for
  every adjacent triple. Therefore acceptance validates one supplied encoded
  Path witness for one supplied finite graph. It does not construct/decide every
  Lean Path and does not certify the whole OpenGraph.
  Mandatory assumptions: the supplied state/port/kind encodings are faithful;
  state equality is the intended equality; graph.step is total and Bool-valued
  on supplied triples; graph.states is the complete finite carrier in scope.
  graph.step is graph data, not a proof oracle, because every edge obligation is
  recomputed. The checker must not accept a separate `path_is_valid` Bool.

Q3. Exact marker test/oracle design
  ACCEPT the proposed design with the following non-circular layout.
  - New checker signature conceptually checks
    check_marker_classification(markers, candidate::Symbol)::Bool.
  - It rejects unknown candidate Symbols, derives the Lean branch condition from
    the four raw Bool fields without calling classify_action_markers, and compares
    against the decoded candidate.
  - A literal 16-row table in test/test_markers.jl supplies the expected Symbol
    for every Bool^4 input. The table must call neither classifier nor checker to
    derive expected values.
  - For every row, assert the classifier result and checker acceptance, then
    replace the candidate by a different valid class and assert rejection.
    Also test an unknown Symbol rejection.
  - The exact sensitivity registry gets one positive case and one mutated-candidate
    negative case for the new contract. It must not use the checker to generate
    its expected candidate.
  This decides the complete finite input graph of Lean classify and avoids the
  classifier-as-its-own-oracle failure.

